    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Luma Stays needed to make a large accommodation catalogue feel easy to
        explore while supporting two distinct user journeys. Customers needed to
        compare venues and book with confidence; venue managers needed practical
        tools for creating, reviewing, and maintaining listings. Both
        experiences had to feel like parts of the same product.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Accommodation decisions involve many competing details: location,
            price, facilities, guest capacity, availability, and trust. The
            interface had to surface the right information at each step without
            crowding cards or making the booking page feel intimidating.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Support quick browsing without hiding meaningful venue details</li>
            <li>Keep search and filters close to the results they affect</li>
            <li>Make booking actions visible while preserving space for
              decision-making content</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-lumastays-explore-600.webp"
            data-src="design-lumastays-explore-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="411"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Luma Stays explore design showing search, filters, and accommodation cards"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Search and filtering remain connected
            to the venue grid</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Designed venue cards around the details users need for an initial
              comparison: image, title, location, capacity, rating, and price
            </li>
            <li>
              Grouped description, facilities, practical details, and booking
              controls into clear areas so the venue page supports both
              evaluation and action
            </li>
            <li>
              Used a restrained travel-inspired palette, generous spacing, and
              consistent card and form patterns to keep a content-heavy
              interface calm
            </li>
            <li>
              Planned responsive layouts around task priority, allowing
              secondary content to move without separating users from the next
              important action
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-lumastays-booking-flow-600.webp"
            data-src="design-lumastays-booking-flow-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="411"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Luma Stays venue detail design showing information, guest selection, and booking controls"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Venue information and booking
            controls share one clear decision flow</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block">
          <h2>Role-based flow</h2>
          <p>
            Customers and venue managers share authentication, navigation, and
            profile patterns, but their goals are different. I kept public
            browsing and booking focused on discovery, while manager actions are
            introduced only when ownership makes them relevant.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Customer profiles prioritise upcoming and previous bookings</li>
            <li>Manager profiles surface owned venues and listing-management
              actions</li>
            <li>Shared components preserve consistency across both account
              experiences</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-lumastays-manager-dashboard-600.webp"
            data-src="design-lumastays-manager-dashboard-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="401"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Luma Stays manager dashboard design showing profile information and managed venues"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Manager dashboard with owned venues
            and account context</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The final design supports discovery, venue evaluation, booking, profile
        management, and listing ownership without making the product feel
        fragmented. The completed application received a
        <strong>Grade A</strong>. This project strengthened how I translate
        complex requirements into reusable interaction patterns and how I design
        related journeys for users with different goals.
      </p>
    </div>

